#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:1
msgid "Feature Name: `rust_foundation_maintainer_fund`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:2
msgid "Start Date: 2026-02-23"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:3
msgid ""
"RFC PR: [rust-lang/rfcs#3931](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3931)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:5
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:8
msgid ""
"This RFC defines the relationship between the Rust Foundation Maintainer "
"Fund (RFMF) and the open-source Rust project. The RFMF is a dedicated fund "
"used to support Rust maintenance: open-ended, multiplicative work that "
"improves Rust and its codebase."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:10
msgid ""
"The Leadership Council has a Project Priorities budget, which is used to "
"fund various initiatives, such as travel grants or program management. RFMF "
"funds will be directed to this budget, but they will be dedicated to "
"activities that direct funding to individual maintainers. This includes the "
"existing program management program and would include the proposed Project "
"Grants Program ([RFC 3919](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3919)), "
"which provides modest stipends to recognize and support existing "
"contributors. It will also include a third program, the _Maintainer in "
"Residence (MiR) program_, proposed by this RFC."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:12
msgid ""
"The Maintainer in Residence program is dedicated to hiring long-term "
"maintainers and funding their maintenance work in full. Maintainers' in "
"Residence time is split between priorities guided by the teams they are "
"supporting and priorities of their own choosing within the project."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:14
msgid ""
"Selecting Maintainers in Residence is a collaboration between the Foundation "
"and a \"Funding team\" whose members are appointed by the Leadership Council "
"or directly by the top-level teams. This Funding team will weigh the set of "
"applications against the project's needs and priorities."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:16
msgid ""
"The Funding team is additionally charged with ensuring the program's overall "
"success. When sponsors contribute undirected funding, they are investing in "
"the Rust project as a whole — and the project should meet them in good "
"faith. Project teams receiving support from the program are expected to help "
"the Funding team manage sponsor relations, e.g., by meeting with sponsors or "
"providing other reasonable sponsor benefits."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:20
msgid ""
"This RFC was jointly written by the [RFMF Design Committee](https://github."
"com/rust-lang/team/blob/0acc660a6bce2b9166362d4bfcbed872508085a6/teams/rfmf-"
"design-committee.toml)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:22
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:25
msgid ""
"The Rust Foundation is establishing a Maintainer Fund to collect "
"sponsorships and provide long-term funding for Rust maintenance. Funds "
"raised through the RFMF are dedicated to funding Rust maintainers under the "
"supervision of the Leadership Council. The Leadership Council commissioned "
"this RFC to recommend how those funds should be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:27
msgid ""
"We recommend that the funds be directed into the Project Priorities budget "
"with the restriction that they be used only for programs that fund Rust "
"maintainers to do maintenance work (which includes project management). "
"Dedicating the funds helps ensure that the \"sales pitch\" is clear: "
"donations given to the RFMF will go directly into a maintainer's pocket."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:29
msgid ""
"We further recommend that the Leadership Council create a \"Maintainer in "
"Residence\" program to augment the existing project management program and "
"the grants program proposed in [RFC 3919](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/3919). Maintainers in Residence are maintainers who are paid to work on "
"a full-time or substantial part-time basis to maintain some part of the "
"project."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:31
msgid "Long-term maintenance is the biggest gap we found"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:33
msgid ""
"In preparing this recommendation, we interviewed team leads across the "
"Project. The message was clear: _\"what's needed is people with the focus to "
"drive longer-scale projects.\"_ Volunteer maintenance can keep the lights "
"on, but larger-scale work stalls because nobody has the sustained focus to "
"push it through. As one (volunteer) team lead said, _\"All the time that "
"reviewers have goes into reviewing, triaging, and so on, and then the "
"interesting longer-term projects just fall under the table.\"_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:35
msgid ""
"The rust-analyzer and Clippy experiences show what funded presence makes "
"possible, and what happens when it disappears:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:37
msgid ""
"When a funded reviewer was working on r-a, the PR backlog stayed around 10. "
"After that person changed jobs, it climbed to over 110: _\"solving the "
"review problem definitely requires money I think, there's no big question "
"there.\"_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:38
msgid ""
"Short-term grants help but aren't enough. The Clippy team received "
"Foundation grants that let _\"one person work almost full time on Clippy, "
"but it was only for 6 months — it was hard to make long-term plans.\"_"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:40
msgid "The problems teams describe require sustained, long-term presence."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:42
msgid "Sponsors come in many shapes and sizes"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:44
msgid "We expect three kinds of support."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:46
msgid ""
"First, small-dollar donations from individuals and organizations that value "
"Rust and want to support its long-term health without any particular "
"expectations. The Foundation will help get the word out via funding drives "
"and PR campaigns."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:48
msgid ""
"Second, when the Foundation takes in directed funding towards a particular "
"goal, best practice will be to direct a percentage of that work into the "
"RFMF, providing another revenue stream for maintenance work. (See the "
"[Future Possibilities](#future-possibilities) section for more discussion in "
"this direction.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:50
msgid ""
"The third category is companies that employ developers or contributors "
"working full-time to improve Rust. These companies are invested in Rust "
"development, but their contributors' work still needs to be reviewed and "
"landed by experienced maintainers. These companies may also need help "
"resolving upstream bugs or limitations that are being hit internally."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:52
msgid "Larger sponsors want predictability; all sponsors need to show impact"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:54
msgid ""
"Sponsoring the maintainer fund is a way for companies to ensure the "
"maintenance layer their contributors depend on stays healthy. An alternative "
"is for the company to hire internal staff to do that role, but beyond being "
"more expensive, experience has shown that \"in house\" maintainer roles at "
"companies are difficult to sustain. Maintenance activities don't advance any "
"single company's goals, so they're hard to justify in a performance review "
"and vulnerable to restructuring when priorities shift."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:56
msgid ""
"For the fund to be sustainable, sponsors also need to be able to report the "
"impact of their contributions. This means the project needs to treat "
"demonstrating impact as a whole-project responsibility, not something that "
"falls on Maintainers in Residence alone. See [the sponsor benefits section]"
"(#sponsor-benefits) for concrete details on what we envision."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:58
msgid ""
"The Project has visibility into needs that aren't always apparent from the "
"outside"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:60
msgid ""
"The fund is structured so that, by default, the Rust project, rather than "
"the sponsors themselves, selects which maintainers to hire and which areas "
"they should focus on. This allows us to aggregate smaller donations and put "
"them to good use. It also means that less visible areas of the project, such "
"as moderation or infrastructure, will be easier to support, as project "
"members are aware of those needs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:62
msgid ""
"But some sponsors will want to fund maintenance in particular areas, and "
"that's ok too"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:64
msgid ""
"Although the default is for the project to pick the area of focus for a MiR, "
"we do allow the Funding team the latitude to offer involvement in area "
"selection as a sponsor benefit at higher tiers. The intention is to permit a "
"company that has a strong need on a particular area to fund a maintainer in "
"that area, if they are willing."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:66
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:69
msgid ""
"The RFMF collects sponsorships from companies and individuals. Funds support "
"project grants, project management, and the Maintainer in Residence program. "
"Maintainers in Residence are experienced, self-directed maintainers who do "
"the work that keeps Rust healthy. They participate in team discussions, "
"review PRs, mentor newcomers, and work on what the team needs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:71
msgid "Design axioms"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:73
msgid "Not one size fits all"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:75
msgid ""
"Maintainers have a wide variety of needs and no one program will work for "
"everyone. We allow RFMF funds to be used for any kind of program that "
"directly pays maintainers for maintenance work (e.g., project grants from "
"[RFC 3919](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3919), the MiR program "
"defined in this RFC, the LC's project management program, or other future "
"programs that may be added)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:77
msgid "The MiR is a collaboration between the Project and the Foundation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:79
msgid ""
"Neither the Foundation nor the Project can operate the MiR program on their "
"own. The Foundation has a bank account, legal entity, and operational "
"capacity; the Project has knowledge of team health and needs. The Foundation "
"is the incoming channel by which most sponsors arrive; the Project governs "
"the codebase that sponsors want to support. This RFC proposes that both "
"project members (the Funding team) and Foundation staff jointly make major "
"decisions. This is a partnership, not a handoff."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:81
msgid ""
"The Funding team owns the RFMF program's success, but they can't do it alone"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:83
msgid ""
"Together with Foundation staff, the Funding team owns sponsor relations and "
"the success of the RFMF program. As the team that selects maintainers and "
"understands project needs, they're best positioned to communicate with "
"sponsors about outcomes and priorities. However, they need support from the "
"broader project, particularly those areas benefiting from a MiR."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:85
msgid "Maintainers are team members"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:87
msgid ""
"Maintainer in Residence candidates must be established members of the Rust "
"Project who either are already members of the relevant team(s) or have been "
"approved by the team(s) to become a member upon starting their MiR role. "
"They need the permissions required for the work — reviewing PRs, championing "
"goals, and performing actions limited to official team members. This is a "
"hard requirement, not just an expectation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:89
msgid ""
"For candidates who are not yet members of the target team, the team must "
"confirm they are willing to add the candidate as a member. In cases where a "
"team is defunct, the parent team(s) can invite the candidate to join and "
"help revive the team."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:91
msgid ""
"Funded maintainers are not a separate class of contributor — they're project "
"members who can now commit sustained time to team responsibilities."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:93
msgid "Sponsor benefits"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:96
msgid ""
"RFMF sponsors typically contribute to a general fund and don't direct where "
"the money goes or who gets hired. Every contribution helps fund the "
"sustained maintenance that keeps Rust healthy. All sponsors receive public "
"recognition and visibility into how funds are being used through regular "
"public reports."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:98
msgid ""
"To encourage larger contributions or year-over-year commitment, the Funding "
"team can also establish sponsorship tiers where sponsors receive particular "
"benefits."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:100
msgid "Possible benefits associated with higher tiers"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:102
msgid ""
"This RFC does not specify the precise tiers or benefits associated with "
"those tiers. Instead, we give examples of the _kinds_ of benefits we "
"anticipate. The Funding team is free to choose these or other benefits that "
"are similar in kind. A good rule of thumb is _\"could the company simply "
"hire a person to do this, presuming they could find someone with the "
"requisite team membership?\"_ -- if so, it's a reasonable benefit to offer."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:104
msgid ""
"**Sponsor meetings.** The Foundation builds a community of sponsoring "
"organizations that meets with project leadership (Leadership Council, team "
"leads) and Maintainers in Residence a few times a year to discuss project "
"direction, sponsor experiences, and pain points. Project leadership gains "
"insight into the needs of major Rust users; sponsors get visibility into the "
"roadmap and the opportunity to hear from other Rust-adopting companies. The "
"frequency of such meetings may depend on the level of sponsorship."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:105
msgid ""
"**Impact reporting.** Regular reports on what funded maintainers are working "
"on, progress on Project Goals, and how the program is contributing to Rust's "
"health. These reports are prepared with help from the program management "
"team and made publicly available."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:106
msgid ""
"**Prioritized review and bug fixes.** Sponsors can reach out to the "
"Foundation or project contacts about PRs or bugs that need attention, up to "
"a certain frequency that is dependent on funding tier. This provides the "
"sponsor with a form of \"insurance\" that they will get help resolving "
"priority issues they encounter with Rust; however, this prioritization "
"should be limited to bug fixes or PR reviews, not to larger feature "
"_development_, and is in no way a promise that a PR will be _merged_ (simply "
"reviewed)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:107
msgid ""
"**Prioritization for goal championing.** Sponsors may suggest that teams use "
"a MiR on that team as a champion for project goals important to them. Teams "
"and their members are encouraged to consider these suggestions but they are "
"not obligated to take them."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:108
msgid ""
"**Area preferences.** If a sponsor or group of sponsors is willing to fund "
"the entire cost of a MiR but only in a specific area, the Funding team could "
"work with them to find a candidate for that particular area."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:109
msgid ""
"For example, if a sponsor would specifically like to fund a cargo or rustfmt "
"maintainer, the Funding team could work with them to make that happen. The "
"role would still be a MiR like any other, following the same processes."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:111
msgid "What is not allowed as a benefit"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:113
msgid ""
"_What sponsors do not get:_ the ability to unilaterally direct a "
"maintainer's work, pick who gets added to project teams, or otherwise bypass "
"project processes."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:115
msgid ""
"Selection process is driven by a team within the project, supported by "
"Foundation staff"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:117
msgid ""
"When funding is available, the Funding team and Foundation put out an open "
"call for applications. Where appropriate, the Funding team may also "
"proactively reach out to potential applicants to encourage them to apply, if "
"they may be a good fit for areas the project needs. The Funding team and "
"Foundation staff review applications, consider the project's needs, and then "
"the Foundation makes offers to the strongest candidates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:119
msgid "The Funding team owns the project's long-term success"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:121
msgid ""
"The Funding team owns the program's overall success. They keep up with teams "
"to understand where support is needed and how well the program is working; "
"they can adjust aspects of the program to make it work better over time."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:123
msgid ""
"The Leadership Council as well as the teams benefitting from the work of "
"Maintainers in Residence are expected to support the Funding teams' efforts "
"(e.g., by meeting with sponsors upon request or otherwise helping out with "
"the sponsor benefits described above)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:125
msgid "What Maintainers in Residence do"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:127
msgid ""
"Maintainers in Residence split their time between team priorities and "
"individual priorities of their own choosing within their area of focus. The "
"exact balance varies depending on the individual, their experience, and the "
"needs of the team — the important thing is that both team-directed and self-"
"directed work are expected. This is about \"team-directed vs. self-directed,"
"\" not \"maintenance vs. features.\" The Python Software Foundation's "
"experience after nearly five years confirms that focusing purely on team-"
"directed needs and multiplicative maintenance can be very draining; giving "
"time for self-directed projects \"made all the difference\" in satisfaction "
"(see [Prior Art](#prior-art))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:129
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:132
msgid "The Funding team's day-to-day responsibilities"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:134
msgid ""
"The Funding team's role is to keep a pulse on the project and work with the "
"Foundation to select which maintainers to hire. Its core responsibilities "
"are:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:136
msgid ""
"**Staying in regular contact with teams** — meeting regularly with team "
"leads and members to understand their needs, health, and where support would "
"have the most impact."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:137
msgid ""
"These meetings can be helpful for purposes other than hiring a MiR: the "
"Funding team may be able to connect the team with Foundation resources to "
"resolve a situation."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:138
msgid ""
"**Working with the Foundation to select MiR candidates** — when positions "
"become available, evaluating applicants and selecting candidates who'll have "
"the most overall impact based on project needs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:139
msgid ""
"**Collecting feedback on how well the program and the MiRs are working** — "
"as the team responsible for selecting which maintainers to hire, the Funding "
"team is also the team responsible for fielding feedback on how well those "
"decisions work out and making adjustments as needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:141
msgid ""
"The Foundation supports the Funding team with logistics. The Foundation "
"issues contracts or manages employment."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:143
msgid ""
"To ensure the membership reflects the will and needs of the teams of the "
"Project overall, new members will be appointed to the Funding team for fixed "
"terms (e.g., one year at a time) which can be renewed.  Either the council "
"will make these appointments or the members will be directly appointed by "
"the top-level teams.  This will be decided when the council adopts the team "
"charter."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:145
msgid ""
"_Unresolved question:_ The Funding team may also take on the duties of the "
"Grants team proposed in [RFC 3919](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/3919). See the [Unresolved Questions](#unresolved-questions) section."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:147
msgid "Funding team affiliation limits"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:149
msgid ""
"Funding team members must not disproportionately come from any one company, "
"legal entity, or closely related set of legal entities, to avoid impropriety "
"or the appearance of impropriety. If the Funding team has 5 or fewer "
"representatives, no more than 1 representative may have any given "
"affiliation; if the Funding team has 6 or more representatives, no more than "
"2 representatives may have any given affiliation. (This affiliation limit "
"comes from [RFC 3392](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3392-leadership-"
"council.html#limits-on-representatives-from-a-single-companyentity), and the "
"Funding team charter should import the definition of affiliations and the "
"processes around handling them from there. The Leadership Council may update "
"this policy through its normal decision-making process.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:151
msgid "The Funding team owns the health of the RFMF program"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:153
msgid ""
"The Funding team has ownership of the RFMF's long-term health. They need to "
"be responsive to both project needs as well as demonstrating return on "
"investment to sponsors."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:155
msgid "Application and vetting process"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:157
msgid ""
"The process of hiring a new Maintainer in Residence begins with an open call "
"for applications. Putting out a broad call for applications helps to surface "
"needs and candidates the Funding team might not have identified on its own."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:159
msgid ""
"Applicants do not have to be a member of a Rust team. However, to be "
"accepted, the team must be willing to grant the person membership. "
"Therefore, applicants should be people who are experienced contributors to "
"the project and, ideally, either a member of the teams they expect to help "
"as MiR or members of some other Rust team."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:161
msgid ""
"Applicants provide (1) their background and experience — both within the "
"Rust project and professionally; (2) their availability (full-time, part-"
"time, etc); and (3) a high-level description of the kind of work they would "
"like to do. This description can be quite general (e.g., \"maintain "
"rustfmt\") but could also be specific (e.g., \"split project `foo` into "
"multiple independent libraries `bar` and `baz`\")."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:163
msgid "The Funding team prioritizes applications based on:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:165
msgid ""
"conversations with team leads and team members to assess what support is "
"most urgently needed;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:166
msgid "the applicant's history with the project;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:167
msgid "any specific work that was proposed in the application;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:168
msgid ""
"the applicant's availability and whether it suffices for the tasks they "
"expect to perform;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:169
msgid "the results of interviews or conversations with the applicant; and"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:170
msgid ""
"any information the moderation team chooses to share regarding the "
"applicant's conduct."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:172
msgid ""
"The Funding team works with the Foundation to select from the applicant pool "
"and to extend offers. The Funding team is looking for maintainers that have "
"technical depth in the relevant area, community standing and trust within "
"the Project, and sustained work orientation (a track record that suggests "
"they'll thrive in a role focused on reviews, mentorship, unblocking, and the "
"kind of long-term technical work that requires deep context)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:174
msgid "Working arrangements should be substantial and stable"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:176
msgid ""
"The precise terms of the working arrangement are not defined by this RFC. "
"Whether achieved through contracts, employment, or other means, the goal is "
"to create a stable environment that allows maintainers to focus on their "
"work in a sustained, year-over-year fashion. The important points are that "
"the arrangement is substantial and stable:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:178
msgid ""
"By _substantial_, we mean that the compensated time is either full-time or "
"significant part-time. Some areas may not require a full person's time; it "
"is fine to have one person cover two areas, or two people each contribute "
"part-time to a single area, so long as there is enough concentrated time to "
"build and maintain deep context. This latitude also allows us to accommodate "
"maintainers who are not interested in a full-time role."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:179
msgid ""
"By _stable_, we mean that it is expected to continue (or be renewed) as long "
"as both sides are satisfied and funding is available."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:181
msgid "We recommend a flat pay structure to start"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:183
msgid ""
"We recommend starting with a single flat rate or small fixed set of flat "
"rates (e.g. 2-3) rather than individually negotiated compensation. Flat "
"rates keep the program simple, avoids the perception that some maintainers "
"are valued more than others for equivalent work, and removes the "
"disadvantage that individual negotiation creates for people who tend to "
"undersell themselves. The rates should be publicly advertised as part of any "
"call for applications."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:185
msgid ""
"That said, the Funding team and Foundation may adjust the compensation "
"approach over time as the program learns what works — including moving to "
"multiple bands, cost-of-living adjustments, or individually negotiated rates "
"if that proves necessary. The Funding team and Foundation determine the "
"specific rate(s)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:187
msgid "Publish compensation rate for MiRs"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:189
msgid ""
"The compensation rate should be published as part of the open call for "
"applications so that prospective applicants and the broader community can "
"see what the program offers. The identities of Maintainers in Residence and "
"the areas they support are also public. Individual totals — hours worked, "
"total compensation received — are not published, though aggregate program "
"spending may be included in impact reports."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:191
msgid "Expectations placed on Maintainers in Residence"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:193
msgid ""
"Maintainers in Residence are expected to spend 100% of their funded time "
"working to improve the Rust project. That funded time can be split between:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:195
msgid ""
"**Team priorities** — items that are prioritized by the team(s) that the "
"maintainer was specifically hired to contribute to. This includes reviews, "
"mentoring, bug-fixing, triage, and larger development work like refactors or "
"subsystem rewrites."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:196
msgid "**Self-selected items** — work of the individual's choosing."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:198
msgid ""
"Experience with the Python Developer-in-Residence program suggests that for "
"long-term satisfaction, it's important that maintainers be given time to "
"pursue self-selected projects. We've also seen over time that maintainers "
"often develop good instincts for what would generally benefit Rust, and thus "
"self-selected \"passion projects\" can turn into some of Rust's most "
"impactful features."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:200
msgid ""
"The split between team priorities and self-selected work will depend on the "
"individual. The Funding team should monitor and make sure that team "
"priorities are being adequately served, while also ensuring that MiRs have "
"the opportunity to pursue self-selected work. If both cannot be done, that "
"likely indicates the need for another MiR in that area."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:202
msgid "Maintainers in Residence are also expected to:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:204
msgid ""
"respond to reasonable requests from the Funding team on behalf of sponsors;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:205
msgid ""
"keep records of their activity as needed by the Funding team (see below);"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:206
msgid "resolve time-critical issues such as urgent bugs;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:207
msgid ""
"champion Project Goals supported by their respective teams, even if they "
"themselves might not have championed that goal as an individual;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:208
msgid "work with the Project to ensure their work gets regularly reported on;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:209
msgid "remain a member of the Project and relevant teams, in good standing."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:211
msgid "Reporting and impact visibility"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:213
msgid ""
"The Funding team may request that MiR collect data on their activity so that "
"they can prepare impact reports and other material for sponsors. Whenever "
"possible, however, the Funding team should work with other teams (e.g., the "
"Goals or Content teams) to handle the creation of that material, so that the "
"MiR can focus primarily on maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:215
msgid ""
"The expectation is that a typical MiR could satisfy these requirements by "
"registering project goals for their major initiatives, posting regular "
"updates, and periodic meetings with their manager. Other teams would "
"supplement this work. For example, the Content team may opt to interview a "
"MiR and prepare a blog post covering their work, and the Funding team might "
"gather Github activity automatically to quantify things like PR reviews or "
"issue triage."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:217
msgid ""
"The Funding team collects feedback (positive or negative) on MiR performance"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:219
msgid ""
"Feedback on MiR performance (positive or negative) can be directed privately "
"to the Funding team. The Funding team will also periodically seek feedback "
"from the project proactively. The Funding team and the manager at the "
"Foundation will convey feedback to the MiR at regular intervals. Feedback, "
"particularly negative feedback, is private and should not be shared publicly "
"without permission from the MiR."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:221
msgid ""
"When negative feedback is received, the Funding team will gather context and "
"work with the MiR manager (see next section) to resolve the concern. "
"Typically this means a conversation that brings about a change in behavior. "
"In extreme cases, this might include performance management options like "
"terminating the arrangement or opting not to renew."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:223
msgid "The Manager works with the Funding team to communicate feedback"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:225
msgid ""
"The _manager_ for a MiR is charged with making sure they are well taken care "
"of. They should meet regularly with the MiR, monitor their workload to "
"ensure that it is balanced and they are not being given either too many or "
"too few responsibilities. They should also have performance conversations at "
"regular intervals to give the MiR a sense for whether they are doing well. "
"Finally, if negative feedback has been received, they should have a clear "
"conversation with MiR to set expectations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:227
msgid ""
"If the program grows to large number of MiRs, however, we recommend that the "
"Leadership Council use some portion of the RFMF funds to hire a dedicated "
"manager who would work closely with the Funding team (see [Who manages "
"Maintainers in Residence after they're hired?](#who-manages-maintainers-in-"
"residence-after-theyre-hired))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:229
msgid "Moderation concerns about a MiR"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:231
msgid ""
"The Moderation team is encouraged to inform the Funding team and/or "
"Foundation about any reported issues relevant to a MiR's conduct."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:233
msgid ""
"Code of Conduct violations that result in removal from the Project will make "
"a Maintainer in Residence no longer eligible to continue in their role. Team "
"membership is a hard requirement for the role; a maintainer who is no longer "
"a project member cannot continue as a Maintainer in Residence. From the "
"project's perspective, the role ends immediately; the legal working "
"arrangement however will end later, as described in the next section."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:235
msgid "Terminating a working arrangement"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:237
msgid ""
"As would be typical in any employment or contracting relationship, a working "
"arrangement might end if:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:239
msgid "there is not adequate funding available to continue the position;"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:240
msgid ""
"the maintainer is not performing adequately or has been removed from their "
"role due to Code of Conduct violations; or"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:241
msgid "there is an urgent need in another area and funds must be redirected."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:243
msgid ""
"Redirecting a well-performing maintainer's position to a different area "
"should not be done lightly. Sustained presence is the core value proposition "
"of the program — startup overhead is real, context takes time to build, and "
"the problems this program targets require continuity. Before redirecting a "
"position, the Funding team should consider whether the existing MiR could "
"help in the new area as well, if the relevant team agrees. The Funding team "
"should strive to grow the program to meet new needs rather than redirecting "
"existing positions."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:245
msgid "Termination period is required"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:247
msgid ""
"Whatever their structure or the legal limits, working arrangements must "
"include a termination period or severance payment to ease the transition. "
"This is needed to ensure that the moderation team can make decisions without "
"having to account for the impact of someone suddenly losing their funding in "
"a preciptious fashion."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:249
msgid "Team requests on a MiR"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:251
msgid ""
"Teams should feel free to ask a MiR to take on high-priority work that "
"nobody else can tackle (championing a goal, driving a critical refactor, "
"clearing a review backlog)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:253
msgid ""
"However, teams should also bear in mind that MiR do not have infinite "
"capacity. MiR can feel free to decline to work on team priorities past a "
"certain point. The MiR manager and Funding team will help to negotiate this "
"balance as needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:255
msgid "Frequently asked questions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:258
msgid ""
"What's the relationship between the Rust Project and the Rust Foundation?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:260
msgid ""
"The Rust programming language is developed by the Rust Project, a community "
"of volunteers organized into teams and governed by the Leadership Council. "
"The Rust Foundation is a separate nonprofit organization that supports the "
"Project — it holds funds, employs staff, and handles legal and operational "
"matters, but does not govern the language or its development. This RFC "
"defines how these two entities collaborate to fund maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:262
msgid "What does it look like to have a Maintainer in Residence on my team?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:264
msgid ""
"In one sense, the same as having any other team member. They show up in "
"reviews, participate in design discussions, mentor newcomers, and work on "
"what the team needs. The PSF's experience after nearly five years is that "
"the Developer in Residence does roughly 50/50 maintenance and proactive "
"work, and the role feels like \"just another team member\" rather than an "
"outside presence (see [Prior Art](#prior-art))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:266
msgid ""
"Unlike a volunteer, however, MiR are expected to put some portion of their "
"time towards team priorities. Whereas before, if there was an urgent task, a "
"team had to hope that somebody had the capacity to pick it up, they can now "
"direct sustained effort where it's needed most. This is a new capability."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:268
msgid "Why not a flexible contractor pool instead of long-term maintainers?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:270
msgid ""
"Context and trust take time to build. The maintenance problems we heard "
"about in team lead interviews (review backlogs, cross-team blocking, complex "
"refactors that need months of sustained attention) require sustained "
"presence, not project-scoped interventions. Contractors for scoped work is a "
"valid model, but it's a different program solving a different problem."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:272
msgid "Who manages Maintainers in Residence after they're hired?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:275
msgid ""
"The Funding team's role ends with the recommendation. Someone needs to "
"synthesize feedback from the Project and make the call on performance. There "
"are two main options:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:277
msgid ""
"**Foundation manages (current RFC position).** Management and performance "
"feedback are skills, and the Foundation has professional staff experienced "
"in them. This shouldn't be a heavy lift early on — we're selecting "
"experienced, self-directed maintainers who are unlikely to have significant "
"performance issues. The Foundation gathers input from the Project (team "
"leads, collaborators) and synthesizes it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:279
msgid ""
"**Project manages.** The Project has deeper technical context to evaluate "
"whether work is landing well. But Project-side management means either a "
"volunteer committee handles it (likely unskilled at management, outside the "
"Funding team's competency) or a dedicated person is hired for it (high "
"overhead relative to the program size, especially early on)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:281
msgid ""
"In practice, we expect this to be a phased approach. While the program has "
"relatively few maintainers, the Foundation provides management skill and the "
"Funding team provides feedback as an input. As the program grows, a "
"dedicated support role may emerge — someone who meets with MiRs regularly, "
"helps them build a portfolio of their work, and serves as the point of "
"contact when teams have concerns. Whether that role lives within the "
"Foundation, the Project, or somewhere in between is a question that becomes "
"more important at scale and can be revisited as the program learns what it "
"needs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:283
msgid "What if a Maintainer in Residence underperforms?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:285
msgid ""
"See the [\"Who manages MiR\"](#who-manages-maintainers-in-residence-after-"
"theyre-hired) question."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:287
msgid "What about people who only want to work part time?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:289
msgid ""
"Maintainers in Residence can work substantial part-time — the key "
"requirement is enough concentrated time to build and maintain deep context, "
"not necessarily a 40-hour week. Some areas may not need a full person's "
"time, and it's fine to have one person cover two areas or two people each "
"contribute part-time to a single area. For contributors who want lighter-"
"touch support, the LC's Project Grants Program ([RFC 3919](https://github."
"com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3919)) is designed for exactly that. The two "
"programs are complementary: grants support a broad base of contributors; the "
"RFMF funds sustained maintenance work from people with deep context."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:291
msgid "What about sponsors who want to pay for a particular item to get done?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:293
msgid ""
"That's outside the scope of the RFMF, which is undirected funding — sponsors "
"contribute to a general fund and the Leadership Council decides how to "
"deploy it. A companion effort, the proposed Project Goals Funding program "
"(see \\[Future possibilities\\]\\[\\#future-possibilities\\]), is designed "
"for exactly this: sponsors direct funding at specific Project Goals, and the "
"Foundation issues grants to advance that work. Sponsors seeking that level "
"of direction should look there rather than the RFMF."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:295
msgid ""
"Why would a company fund the RFMF instead of hiring a maintainer directly?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:297
msgid ""
"During the [discussion on the RFC](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/3931#r2943144693), the question was raised as to what incentive "
"companies have to pay into the (project-controlled) RFMF versus hiring a "
"maintainer directly. If companies wish to hire maintainers directly, we "
"think that is great. However, our experience is that the incentives at "
"companies tend to reward _contribution_ more than _maintenance_, as "
"contribution has more visible value to the company; but contribution without "
"maintenance doesn't work, and as a result many company's efforts to "
"contribute to Rust wind up stalled. The intention for the RFMF is to give "
"companies a price-effective way to share the cost of maintenance by pooling "
"funds. In so doing, they both help ensure Rust's continued improvement _and_ "
"make it easier for their own contributions to be accepted (since there are "
"maintainers to review them)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:299
msgid "Why are RFMF funds dedicated to maintainers?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:301
msgid ""
"Sponsors contributing to the RFMF want to know their money is going directly "
"to fund maintainers. Dedicating the funds gives the fund a clear value "
"proposition: every dollar goes to paying people who maintain Rust. Without "
"this restriction, the Leadership Council could use RFMF funds for any "
"purpose — travel grants, event sponsorship, infrastructure — all valuable, "
"but harder to explain to a company evaluating its return on investment. "
"Since money is fungible, dedicating RFMF funds to maintainers frees up "
"budget for other purposes."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:303
msgid ""
"The restriction is broad: Maintainers in Residence, project grants, and the "
"program management overhead needed to support them. The Leadership Council "
"determines the specific form. This gives the Council real flexibility while "
"keeping the fund's purpose legible to sponsors."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:305
msgid "What does this RFC deliberately not specify?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:307
msgid ""
"This RFC does not define how the RFMF collects sponsorships, the precise "
"tiers or benefits sponsors receive, the precise pay structure, or other fine-"
"grained details. The Funding team and Foundation can work these out."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:309
msgid ""
"What is an example of something that RFMF funds could _not_ be used for?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:311
msgid ""
"This RFC proposes that RFMF funds are limited to funds that compensate Rust "
"maintainers for time they spend doing maintenance. They could not be used "
"for other Project Priorities budget items such as organizing the Rust All "
"Hands or running a travel grant program."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:313
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:316
msgid "Python Software Foundation: lessons from a mature program"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:318
msgid ""
"The [PSF Developer in Residence program](https://www.python.org/psf/"
"developersinresidence/) started in 2021. After nearly five years, it now "
"funds three maintainers, each sponsored by a specific company (Meta, "
"Bloomberg, and Vercel). Contracts are for 12 months, renewable based on "
"continued sponsor funding. Maintainers are employees or contractors of the "
"PSF, reporting to both the Executive Director and the Steering Council."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:320
msgid ""
"One important lesson that we took from our discussions with the Python "
"Developers in Residence and the Steering Council is the importance of self-"
"directed time. The first Developer in Residence started doing only reviews, "
"backports, and CI, and found that after a year or two, \"there's not much "
"joy in that.\" Allowing feature work alongside maintenance \"made all the "
"difference.\" We've reflected this in the RFMF design: Maintainers in "
"Residence split their time roughly 50/50 between team priorities and "
"individual priorities of their choosing, rather than being assigned purely "
"to maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:322
msgid ""
"One area where we have deviated from Python precedent is in attempting to "
"create a system where most sponsors pool their money into a general fund, "
"rather than funding specific individuals (similar to Zig or the Scala "
"Center, see below). This may be a challenge, as the PSF has found that being "
"able to clearly identify the impact of a sponsors' funding is useful when "
"making the case for renewal. For this reason, we also allow for larger "
"sponsors who wish to sponsor an entire person, rather than putting money "
"into a general pool."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:324
msgid "Django Fellowship: weekly reports and community-focused maintenance"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:326
msgid ""
"The [Django Fellowship program](https://github.com/django/dsf-working-groups/"
"blob/main/active/fellowship.md) has been running since 2014, predating the "
"PSF program and providing the longest track record of any comparable effort. "
"Fellows are contractors (not employees) who post weekly reports. The work is "
"focused on \"housekeeping and community support\": monitoring security "
"email, fixing release blockers, reviewing pull requests, and mentoring new "
"contributors."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:328
msgid "Zig Software Foundation: lean and independent"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:330
msgid ""
"The [ZSF](https://ziglang.org/zsf/) takes a simpler approach: core team "
"members bill hours directly to the foundation. As a 501(c)(3) non-profit "
"founded in 2020, 92% of its spending goes directly to paying contributors, "
"with minimal administrative overhead. It has no big tech companies on its "
"board, an explicit design choice to maintain independence."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:332
msgid ""
"The ZSF model is leaner and more informal than PSF or Django, but also "
"smaller in scale and more dependent on individual large donations. It "
"demonstrates that low-overhead models are possible, but the approach may not "
"scale to the number of maintainers Rust needs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:334
msgid "Scala Center: pool-funded with sponsor engagement"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:336
msgid ""
"The [Scala Center](https://scala.epfl.ch/), housed at EPFL, takes a pool-"
"funded approach that contrasts with the PSF's per-sponsor-per-position "
"model. Corporate sponsors contribute to a general fund at tiered levels and "
"send representatives to quarterly Advisory Board meetings. The Advisory "
"Board makes non-binding recommendations on priorities; the Center's "
"leadership decides on execution and hiring. Sponsors influence direction "
"through discussion but don't direct specific positions or hires."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:338
msgid ""
"Two aspects of the Scala Center model have been particularly influential on "
"this RFC. First, the sponsor meeting structure: sponsors meet regularly with "
"maintainers and with each other, and these meetings have been described as a "
"\"big win\" for selling the program internally. Having sponsor "
"representatives commit to attend regular meetings makes the program legible "
"to upper management. Second, sponsors often value hearing from their peers — "
"other organizations using the language — as much as from the project itself. "
"We encourage the Funding team to recreate this by creating a community of "
"mid-level sponsors."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:340
msgid "Project Grants Program: a related committee model"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:342
msgid ""
"The Project Grants Program ([RFC 3919](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/3919)) proposes a program supporting a handful of contributors with "
"modest monthly stipends. It charters a Grants team (5 members, LC-appointed, "
"organized as a Launching Pad subteam) to select recipients and oversee the "
"program. The RFC explicitly positions itself as \"distinct from, but "
"complementary to\" the MiR: grants are smaller-scale, flexible, Project-"
"controlled support, while the RFMF targets larger-scale, sustained "
"maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:344
msgid ""
"The Grants team's charter overlaps significantly with the Funding team "
"charter we define here — both involve assessing project needs and selecting "
"candidates. The Leadership Council may choose to extend the Grants team with "
"the Funding team's responsibilities rather than creating a separate body, "
"which would avoid fragmenting the Project's attention across multiple "
"committees with overlapping mandates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:346
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:349
msgid "Organizational form of the Funding team"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:352
msgid ""
"This RFC defines the Funding team's charter — its responsibilities — but "
"leaves the organizational structure to the Leadership Council. There are two "
"main options:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:354
msgid ""
"**Merge the Grants team and the Funding team.** The Grants team proposed in "
"[RFC 3919](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3919) already has an LC "
"appointment process, selection infrastructure, and conflict-of-interest "
"policies. We could merge these two teams together. This would mean that "
"serving on the team is significantly more work, but the process of selecting "
"Grant recipients and MiR also share significant overlap."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:356
msgid ""
"**Create a new team.** We could also have two distinct teams, where each "
"team has a narrower charter. This might make it easier to staff the two "
"teams since the workload for any individual team is less."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:358
msgid "Comparing the two teams:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:360
msgid ""
"The Funding team's charter is broader than selecting recipients — it "
"includes staying in regular contact with teams, connecting them to "
"resources, and understanding project health holistically. MiR selection is "
"only one part of that mandate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:361
msgid ""
"There is also a difference in operating cadence: the Grants program has "
"predictable cycles, while the Funding team may need to react promptly when "
"new funding becomes available rather than waiting for the next scheduled "
"round. Current Grants team members may not have signed up for that kind of "
"bandwidth or latency."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:363
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:366
msgid "Extending the vetting service to other funding organizations"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:368
msgid ""
"This RFC defines the interface between the Rust Project and the RFMF "
"specifically, but nothing about the process is inherently RFMF-specific. The "
"Funding team's core service — assessing where dedicated maintainers would "
"have the most impact, evaluating candidates, and collecting performance "
"feedback — could be offered to other funding organizations that want to hire "
"Maintainers in Residence. The value proposition is the same regardless of "
"who's paying: the Project has visibility into which teams are struggling and "
"which candidates have the trust and context to be effective, and funding "
"organizations benefit from that assessment rather than making hiring "
"decisions without it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:370
msgid "Recording MiR affiliations"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:372
msgid ""
"If the Rust Project establishes a mechanism for recording affiliations of "
"team members, Maintainers in Residence could record their RFMF funding as an "
"affiliation. This would make funding relationships visible through the same "
"infrastructure used for employer affiliations."
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:374
msgid "Project Goals Funding program"
msgstr ""

#: src/3931-rfmf-rust-foundation-maintainer-fund.md:376
msgid ""
"The RFMF provides undirected funding — sponsors contribute to a general fund "
"dedicated to funding Rust maintainers, with the Leadership Council deciding "
"the specific form. There are ongoing plans to propose a Project Goals "
"Funding program that would allow sponsors to direct funding at specific "
"Project Goals. Sponsors would choose goals, roadmaps, or application areas "
"to fund, and the Foundation would issue grants to contributors working on "
"that work. A percentage of directed funding would flow to the Leadership "
"Council's Project Priorities budget, where it can be used to fund "
"maintenance, project management, or other activities. Together, the two "
"programs cover the full spectrum of sponsor needs: undirected funding for "
"those who want to support Rust's overall health, and directed funding for "
"those who want to accelerate specific work."
msgstr ""
